--TEST--
`Core\Request::bytes()` answers a binary request body whole, past the NUL and the `0xff` that no `string` could hold — `rule:http-server/buffering-readers-share-the-body-and-streaming-readers-consume-it`, `rule:types/string-is-utf8`, `rule:testing/in-process-request`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A PNG signature with a NUL and an `0xff` after it: octets a `string` cannot
// hold, which is the whole reason this reading exists beside `body()`. They are
// carried in by `Core\Test::request` because a case file crosses as text, so
// `--POST_RAW--` has no spelling for them.
//
// `Core\Test::request` runs this same file again for the request, and in that
// run `Core\Env::context()` is `Request`, so the first branch is the request.
if (Core\Env::context() == Core\Env::Context::Request) {
    // `tainted`, exactly as an arrived request's body is -- nothing about being
    // synthetic changes what the roster declares. The refusal that pins the
    // qualifier is its own case, a plain `bytes` binding being the only thing
    // that can fail over a mark a passing case would widen away.
    tainted bytes $raw = Core\Request::bytes();
    // Whole: the count runs past the NUL a reader treating the body as text
    // would stop at, and the hex says which octets in which order.
    echo "octets ", Core\Bytes::length($raw), "\n";
    echo "hex ", Core\Encoding::toHex($raw), "\n";
} else {
    var $answered = Core\Test::request(
        Core\Http\Method::Post,
        "/hooks",
        {body: Core\Encoding::fromHex("89504e470d0a1a0a00ff")}
    );
    echo $answered->body();
}
--EXPECT--
octets 10
hex 89504e470d0a1a0a00ff
